</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span> </span><span id="S2.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Models</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this study, we evaluate our strategies on the models provided in the JetBrains Competition, including Mellum:4b, Qwen2.5-Coder:7b and Codestral <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>.
<span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mellum:4b</span> is a code-specific model developed by JetBrains that is fine-tuned on real-world code completion scenarios. It supports a range of programming languages and has a context window of 8,192 tokens <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>.
<span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Qwen2.5-Coder:7b</span> was introduced by Alibaba Cloud and the model is part of the Qwen2.5-Coder series. It is built on the Qwen2.5 architecture and fine-tuned on code-based tasks, achieving strong results across benchmarks<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. In the competition setting, the context window was limited to 16,000 tokens.
<span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Codestral:22B</span> is a code model developed by Mistral AI designed with fill-in-the-middle capabilities for code completion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>. Like Qwen2.5-Coder, it has a 16,000 token context window in the competition environment.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">II-C</span> </span><span id="S2.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Code Completion Task</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the effectiveness of repository-level code completion across multiple large language models (LLMs).
The task involves the models generating the missing code segment in a <span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">target file</span> using both the surrounding code and additional code snippets from the repository. The problem is formulated as a Fill-in-the-middle (FIM) task, where the input consists of: 1) <span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prefix: </span>the code preceding the missing segment, 2)
<span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Suffix: </span>the code following the missing segment, and 3) <span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Additional retrieved context: </span>relevant code snippets from the repository.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The models’ objective is to generate a suitable fit for the missing code that bridges the prefix and suffix, leveraging both local and retrieved repository context.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">II-D</span> </span><span id="S2.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Evaluation Metrics</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">JetBrains evaluated the efficiency of the strategy using the <span id="S2.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> average chrF</span> (character n-gram F-score) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> across the three models.
The chrF score evaluates the overlap between the generated completion and the ground truth sequences based on contiguous sequences of characters, rather than whole words <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, which makes it sensitive to crucial differences in code syntax <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS5.6" class="ltx_text">II-E</span> </span><span id="S2.SS5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Baselines</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To show the need for effective context collection strategies to improve the performance of models in code completion, we define a <span id="S2.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">no-context</span> baseline alongside the JetBrains <span id="S2.SS5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">recent</span> baseline (which was only computed for Python in the Practice phase) to compare the results of our retrieval strategies.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S2.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">no-context baseline</span> involves providing the LLMs with only the prefix and suffix surrounding the masked code without any additional repository context.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S2.SS5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">recent baseline</span> involves selecting a <span id="S2.SS5.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">single</span> context file from a list of recently <span id="S2.SS5.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">modified</span> files in the same commit as the missing code that are above ten lines of code; if no qualifying files are found, a random file is selected. Together, these baselines measure the performance of models with no contextual information and when given minimal, randomly selected context as shown in Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ III Searching the Best Approach ‣ Beyond More Context: How Granularity and Order Drive Code Completion Quality" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Searching the Best Approach</span></h2>

<figure id="S3.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE II: </span>Preliminary results of attempted strategies (Practice Phase). We highlight in gray the best results per experiment, and in bold the best overall results.
